## 2. What confidence means

> **In one sentence:** confidence is the probability that the final MERC lands within an agreed tolerance of the true rebuild cost.

The tolerance says how far off the MERC may be and still count as right. It's **asymmetric** on purpose:

- A MERC that's **too low** leaves the home underinsured and leaks premium. Keep this side tight.
- A MERC that's **too high** makes the quote less competitive. This side can be looser.

$$
C \;=\; P\big(-\tau_{\text{over}} \;\le\; \ln(\text{true cost}) - \ln(\text{final MERC}) \;\le\; \tau_{\text{under}}\big)
$$

↳ **In words:** $C$ is the chance that the MERC is no more than $\tau_{\text{under}}$ too low and no more than $\tau_{\text{over}}$ too high.

This document uses $\tau_{\text{under}} = 0.10$ and $\tau_{\text{over}} = 0.15$, about 10% and 15%, for illustration. The real values are a business decision.

> 💡 **Why logs?** On the log scale, a difference of 0.05 is about 5%, and multiplying effects become adding effects. Tables in this document show percentages; the math underneath runs on logs.

