## 3. The idea at a glance

> **In one sentence:** score two versions of the house, one where the member was right and one where they were wrong, and average them.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    FIN["Signal 2<br/>Final MERC + range"]
    PCS["Signal 3<br/>Property Confidence Score"]
    NB["Signal 4<br/>H3 neighborhood estimate"]
    PRE["Signal 1<br/>Prefill MERC + range"]

    RIGHT["If the member was right<br/>weight 1 − q"]
    WRONG["If the member was wrong<br/>weight q"]

    FIN --> RIGHT
    PCS --> RIGHT & WRONG
    NB --> RIGHT & WRONG
    PRE --> WRONG

    RIGHT --> C["Confidence C<br/>weighted average"]
    WRONG --> C
    C --> OUT["Tier + guardrails<br/>+ main driver"]

    classDef signal fill:#F1F5F9,stroke:#94A3B8,color:#0F172A
    classDef version fill:#FFFFFF,stroke:#64748B,color:#0F172A
    classDef result fill:#DBEAFE,stroke:#2563EB,stroke-width:2px,color:#0F172A
    class PRE,FIN,PCS,NB signal
    class RIGHT,WRONG version
    class C,OUT result
```

For each version of the house, the score asks the same three questions:

1. **Where is the true cost probably centered?** Start at that version's MERC, then let the neighborhood estimate pull it a little.
2. **How spread out is it?** Combine Verisk's range with the leftover data risk from the Property Confidence Score.
3. **What's the chance the quote lands within tolerance?** A standard Normal calculation.

The final confidence is the weighted average of the two versions. When the member changed nothing, both versions are the same house and the average is just that one answer.

