## 8. Setting the parameters

> **In one sentence:** every parameter has a documented starting value and a way to replace it with evidence, and none of them requires a model trained on MERC.

| Parameter | Starting value | How to set it properly | Data needed |
|---|:-:|---|---|
| **Tolerances** $\tau_{\text{under}}$, $\tau_{\text{over}}$ | 0.10, 0.15 | Business decision | — |
| **Range coverage** | 90% | Documented assumption | — |
| **Leftover data risk** $\sigma_D$ by PCS | 0.02 to 0.12 | Calibrate from the audit arm (below) | Audit arm + stored prefill and final MERCs |
| **Chance the member is wrong** $q$ | 0.10 | Inspection findings on member-changed attributes (below) | Post-bind inspections |
| **Credibility constant** $k$ | 25 | Bühlmann credibility, per H3 level | Book |
| **Tier cutoffs** | 0.90, 0.70 | Shadow-mode tier mix plus correction rates ([section 9](#9-proving-it-works)) | Shadow-mode log |
| **Guardrails** | 30 homes; $\lvert z \rvert$ > 3 | Review after shadow mode | Shadow-mode log |

### Calibrating the leftover data risk by PCS

The audit arm, a small random share of quotes where **every** material attribute is asked, makes this possible without any extra Verisk calls:

- In the **audit arm**, the move from prefill to final MERC shows how wrong prefill MERCs are **in total**.
- In the **normal flow**, the same move shows how much of that our **usual questions fix**.
- The **difference** is what our usual questions leave behind, which is exactly $\sigma_D$.

$$
\sigma_D^2(\text{PCS}) \;\approx\; \operatorname{Var}_{\text{audit}}\!\big[\,s \mid \text{PCS}\,\big] \;-\; \operatorname{Var}_{\text{normal}}\!\big[\,s \mid \text{PCS}\,\big], \qquad s = \ln\frac{\text{final MERC}}{\text{prefill MERC}}
$$

↳ **In words:** the total error, minus the part our questions fix, leaves the part they don't.

Practical details:

- **Random assignment** to the audit arm is what makes the two groups comparable.
- **Drop obvious data errors** (moves of 3× or more) before computing variances.
- **Sample size:** the difference of two variances is noisy, so aim for a few thousand quotes per PCS level in each group, and pool adjacent levels where data is thin.
- **Keep it monotonic:** a lower PCS should never get *less* leftover risk than a higher one.
- **The approximation assumes** that errors our questions fix and errors they leave behind are independent. That's reasonable when they involve different attributes.

### Calibrating the chance the member is wrong

From post-bind inspections, among quotes where the member **changed** an attribute that drives MERC, measure how often the inspection found the member's value wrong. This uses attribute data only. Start with a single $q$; split it by attribute later if the data supports it.

