## 9. Proving it works

> **In one sentence:** run it silently first, then check that lower tiers really do have more problems, using attribute data.

| Check | What to do | What good looks like |
|---|---|---|
| **Shadow mode** | Score every quote for 4–8 weeks without acting. Log $C$, the tier, the waterfall and the driver. | A stable tier mix |
| **Tier mix** | Set cutoffs so Low is a share review or inspection can handle (for example, 2–4%) | A workable flag rate |
| **Tier separation** | On the audit arm and post-bind inspections, compare attribute correction rates by tier | Correction rates rise clearly from High to Medium to Low |
| **PCS sanity check** | Compare attribute correction rates by PCS level | Lower PCS, more corrections, which confirms the direction assumption |
| **Review outcomes** | Track how often underwriters find a real problem on Low quotes | A meaningful share of Low quotes lead to a correction |
| **Monitoring** | Review quarterly, and after every Verisk cost-data update | No drift |

> ⚠️ **Why the audit arm matters:** in the normal flow we only see corrections on attributes we asked about. The audit arm is the only unbiased view, and it's also what calibrates $\sigma_D$.

